  "analysis": "Network is reachable (YouTube returned 200). No curl/wget, but python3 has urllib. I need to install ffmpeg, tesseract, and yt-dlp. Let me update apt and install ffmpeg + tesseract, and download the yt-dlp binary via python3.",
  "plan": "Run apt-get update, then install ffmpeg and tesseract-ocr via apt. Download yt-dlp standalone binary using python3 urllib. Then verify all tools work.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get update 2>&1 | tail -5\n",
      "duration": 30.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "apt-get update succeeded. Now I need to install ffmpeg, tesseract-ocr, and get yt-dlp. Let me install the apt packages and download the yt-dlp binary in parallel.",
  "plan": "Install ffmpeg and tesseract-ocr via apt-get. Download the yt-dlp standalone binary using python3. These take time, so I'll run them with adequate durations.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get install -y ffmpeg tesseract-ocr 2>&1 | tail -8\n",
      "duration": 60.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "apt-get install failed: not enough free space in /var/cache/apt/archives. The install would need 211MB downloaded + 724MB installed. I need to check available disk space and free up room, then install only what's necessary.",
  "plan": "Check disk space with df. Clean apt cache. Then try installing with --no-install-recommends to reduce footprint, and possibly install tesseract without all recommended packages. Also consider downloading yt-dlp binary which is small.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -h / /tmp /var 2>&1\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get clean; ls -la /var/cache/apt/archives/ | head\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The filesystem reports 0 available and 100% used. apt needs 211MB to download. This is a real constraint. I need to either free up space or find a more efficient approach. Let me investigate what's consuming space and whether I can reclaim it.",
  "plan": "Check disk usage details with du to find large directories to clean. Also verify whether free space is truly 0 by attempting a small write. This will inform whether I can install tools or need a leaner approach.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -h; echo '==='; du -sh /usr /var /tmp /root 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "touch /tmp/_test_write && echo WRITE_OK && rm /tmp/_test_write || echo WRITE_FAIL\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "df reports 0 available across all overlays (957G/984G used), but small writes to /tmp succeed. This is likely an overlay size accounting quirk. I need to verify the real writable capacity before committing to a 724MB install. Let me test with a larger write, since the apt 'no free space' error may be overly conservative.",
  "plan": "Test writing a few hundred MB to /tmp to discover the true limit. If writes work, I can install ffmpeg+tesseract. I'll also try pointing apt's cache elsewhere. First probe real capacity.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "dd if=/dev/zero of=/tmp/_bigtest bs=1M count=200 2>&1 | tail -2; ls -la /tmp/_bigtest; rm -f /tmp/_bigtest\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Writes to /tmp work fine (200MB written successfully) — the df '0 available' is an overlay accounting quirk, not a real limit. apt's pre-download space check was overly conservative. The real capacity is available. I'll retry the install, using --no-install-recommends to minimize footprint, and download the yt-dlp binary separately.",
  "plan": "Retry installing ffmpeg and tesseract-ocr with --no-install-recommends to reduce size. In parallel, download the yt-dlp standalone binary via python3 urllib. Then verify all tools.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends ffmpeg tesseract-ocr 2>&1 | tail -6\n",
      "duration": 60.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The apt 'no free space' error persists even though I proved /tmp can hold 200MB writes — df's overlay reports 0 available but real writes work. This is apt's statvfs-based free-space guard blocking the download. I need to find the apt config option to disable that check. I'll query apt-config for the relevant option name.",
  "plan": "Run `apt-config dump` and grep for the free-space / space option to learn its exact name, then disable it and retry the install. This avoids fighting the spurious space guard since actual capacity is available.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-config dump 2>/dev/null | grep -i -E 'space|free'\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "apt's free-space guard is keyed off statvfs, which reports 0 available for the overlay even though a 200MB write to /tmp succeeded — a classic fuse-overlayfs statvfs quirk. So real capacity exists, but apt refuses to download. I need to confirm the true writable limit and statvfs behavior, then bypass apt's guard (either by fetching .debs myself and dpkg-installing, or by tricking the check). ffmpeg + tesseract are still not installed.",
  "plan": "1) Probe the real writable limit with a larger dd and inspect statvfs on /var/cache. 2) Use `apt-get install --print-uris` to get the list of .deb URLs without triggering the download/space check. 3) Download those .debs with python3 and install via dpkg -i, sidestepping apt's statvfs guard. First, confirm capacity and gather URIs.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "dd if=/dev/zero of=/tmp/_big bs=1M count=900 2>&1 | tail -1; rm -f /tmp/_big\n",
      "duration": 8.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "stat -f /var/cache/apt/archives\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get install --print-uris --no-install-recommends ffmpeg tesseract-ocr > /tmp/uris.txt 2>&1; wc -l /tmp/uris.txt; head -5 /tmp/uris.txt\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Key finding: statvfs on the overlay reports Available: 0 (a fuseblk quirk) even though Free blocks show ~28GB and I successfully wrote 900MB to /tmp. apt/dpkg both key off 'Available' and refuse. So real capacity exists; I just need to bypass the statvfs guard. The clean way is to download the .debs myself and extract them with `dpkg-deb -x` (which does NOT do a disk-space check), then run the binaries via a custom LD_LIBRARY_PATH. First I need the URI list from --print-uris.",
  "plan": "1) Inspect /tmp/uris.txt to confirm the deb URL list. 2) Download all debs via python3 urllib into /tmp/debs. 3) Extract every deb into /tmp/rootfs with dpkg-deb -x. 4) Build LD_LIBRARY_PATH from extracted libs and run ffmpeg/tesseract from that tree. Start by viewing the URI list.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "head -8 /tmp/uris.txt; echo '...'; grep -c \"'\" /tmp/uris.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

